claire: quick thread 11 opened a PR to refresh snippets: emphasize consumer-side retries, show resume-token handling for streaming, and clarify pagination sentinel variations. https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/1012
dan: nice. can you highlight what "resume-token" guidance is? we've had confusion about replays and dedupe.
claire: added a short example for consumers to persist resume_token (not resend partial input) and dedupe server-provided partials. gist below:
```python
# python: persist resume token and dedupe partials
from redwood import Client

client = Client()
seen_ids = set()
resume_token = load_token()  # load from durable store
with client.stream('gpt-pro', prompt='summarize') as s:
    if resume_token:
        s.resume(resume_token)
    for chunk in s:
        if getattr(chunk, 'id', None) in seen_ids:
            continue
        seen_ids.add(getattr(chunk, 'id', None))
        handle(chunk.text)
        save_token(getattr(chunk, 'resume_token', None))
```
maria: excellent. that should help customers who try to naive-retry by replaying inputs. include a one-line caution: do not re-send partial inputs when resuming (can cause duplicates).
kevin: also flagged a subtle pagination area: some endpoints historically returned either null, the empty string, or the sentinel string "<END>". i've added a compact Go example that tolerates all three cases.
```go
// go: pagination defensive handling for null/empty/sentinel
var cursor *string = nil
for {
  resp, err := client.List(ctx, &ListParams{Cursor: cursor})
  if err != nil { return nil, err }
  items = append(items, resp.Items...)
  if resp.Cursor == nil { break }
  if *resp.Cursor == "" || *resp.Cursor == "<END>" { break }
  cursor = resp.Cursor
}
```
claire: the TS snippet shows consumer-side retry w/ jitter + abort signal support (AbortController) so web apps can cancel quickly. paste:
```ts
// TS: stream with AbortController and jittered retry
import { Redwood } from '@redwood/inference'

async function fetchStream(prompt: string, signal?: AbortSignal) {
  const client = new Redwood()
  let tries = 0
  while (tries < 4) {
    try {
      const stream = await client.stream('gpt-pro', { prompt, signal })
      for await (const chunk of stream) {
        console.log(chunk.text)
      }
      return
    } catch (err) {
      if (signal?.aborted) throw err
      const backoff = (Math.random() + 0.5) * Math.pow(2, tries) * 200
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, backoff))
      tries++
      if (tries === 4) throw err
    }
  }
}
```
